<node id="Obscuration">
  <data key="d0">Obscuration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique employed in the offense to shape the operational environment, protect friendly forces, and deceive enemy commanders.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition or effect used to reduce the risk of detection and enemy targeting.&lt;SEP&gt;Obscuration is employed by units when moving without natural cover or the ability to build fortifications to limit damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-279aa3020dae92333d1d26e60241ff57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4240b292090a3a425c9dae95b6a7c26a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operational Environment">
  <data key="d0">Operational Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The setting that is shaped and controlled through the use of obscuration.&lt;SEP&gt;The Operational Environment is understood and shaped by Army special operations forces to support situational awareness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-279aa3020dae92333d1d26e60241ff57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950f5d02c8283b123e3471016b2f74a1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758118</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Friendly Forces">
  <data key="d0">Friendly Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Friendly Forces refer to allied military entities, including ground and air elements, involved in various operations. Their most forward positions are typically indicated by the Forward Line of Own Troops (FLOT). These units primarily conduct defense but may transition to offensive operations upon success. To maintain security, they are protected by obscuration and enabled by intelligence products. Additionally, these military units attempt to prevent the enemy from knowing when they become overextended.

Identification is critical, as positions must be positively identified to prevent friendly fire incidents. Friendly Forces must be distinguished from combatants to avoid such errors. However, enemy forces may attempt to blend with these allied personnel or utilize decoys. Operational challenges include personnel operating in urban areas who may be endangered by weapon penetration or uncontrolled fires. Furthermore, mobility may be compromised if road surfaces are destroyed.

Logistics and support are essential, as these units require insertion, support, and coordination for evacuation priorities. The proximity and support of Friendly Forces significantly affect personnel recovery and evasion challenges. Ultimately, they represent military or allied groups whose status and location dictate various tactical and safety protocols within the battlespace.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-279aa3020dae92333d1d26e60241ff57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c73d56d8e9bfb3038d769d096b30c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da551c8c73e884b03192b52010d81be5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abe3f08fafe8b7389e20d639ffaa8692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16fb9a43ba23b08191569577e00618b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe3e79800d49e6c15cc7d1d7485cab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2039fcc410ab417ea95cf892f406c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32189ba42b4061e42fcfd89b6143133c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b14fece49276b354d02ce9679408699&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446c344294ac1157381c690e0f0b5d6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb60400466a6008ca4dd586ce73558f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ff2850046e68e7092903c98bb6595e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778751499</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enemy Forces">
  <data key="d0">Enemy Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Enemy Forces are defined as opposing military entities or units engaged with friendly forces, designated by the abbreviation "ENY" on the line of contact. These hostile military groups serve as primary targets for reconnaissance, security operations, attack, and intelligence gathering. In offensive operations, commanders seek to defeat, destroy, deceive, or divert Enemy Forces, targeting their movement, maneuver, and counterattacks, sometimes employing obscuration to deceive them. From a defensive perspective, Enemy Forces represent the threat that defending units prepare against, study, and seek to canalize into engagement areas for neutralization, suppression, and destruction. During transitions, friendly units maintain surveillance while Enemy Forces actively employ diverse means, ranging from saboteurs to air assaults, to attack defending elements and disrupt operations.

Tactically, Enemy Forces may utilize urban environments for cover, concealment, movement, and fighting positions. The density and threats posed by these hostile groups significantly impact evasion and recovery operations within urban settings. Adversaries may blend with friendly or neutral forces and exploit US rules of engagement, also employing tactics such as using civilian equipment for military purposes. Regarding mobility vulnerabilities, Enemy Forces are opposing military units that may be rendered unable to move if road surfaces are destroyed. Across all contexts, they remain the target of engagement.&lt;SEP&gt;Enemy Forces are targeted by Army Special Operations Forces through direct action and offensive actions to seize key terrain.&lt;SEP&gt;Enemy forces attempt to disrupt operations by affecting vulnerable targets in rear areas and possess capabilities such as precision fires and ballistic missiles.&lt;SEP&gt;Adversary forces whose capabilities, will, and cohesion are targeted by special operations to reduce effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Forces destroyed by Army special operations forces during armed conflict.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-279aa3020dae92333d1d26e60241ff57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c73d56d8e9bfb3038d769d096b30c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4240b292090a3a425c9dae95b6a7c26a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abe3f08fafe8b7389e20d639ffaa8692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa0cb96393bbc79e8f283b9593a9806&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d61f4b5597bdee4add1bccdba2cbd3ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d85f7f19b9f147f37bc741a7c59d700&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3345760dcbfb2262ee6ddfa43b1677ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14acea44dac3d3616369b19ec0f2146&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c503bd64404512328ab83ccc0ba0997e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2039fcc410ab417ea95cf892f406c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b14fece49276b354d02ce9679408699&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb60400466a6008ca4dd586ce73558f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63ff2850046e68e7092903c98bb6595e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a75fd9bf2d8ffd7deb7994794314ba94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660d19754e7a8b4d28766ae683085446</data>
