  <data key="d2">Diamond is one of the seven movement formations used by commanders depending on mission variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wedge">
  <data key="d0">Wedge</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Wedge is one of the seven movement formations used by commanders depending on mission variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vee">
  <data key="d0">Vee</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Vee is one of the seven movement formations used by commanders depending on mission variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movement Technique">
  <data key="d0">Movement Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Movement techniques are used within movement formations and include traveling, traveling overwatch, and bounding overwatch.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traveling">
  <data key="d0">Traveling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Traveling is one of the three movement techniques used in movement formations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traveling Overwatch">
  <data key="d0">Traveling Overwatch</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Traveling overwatch is one of the three movement techniques used in movement formations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bounding Overwatch">
  <data key="d0">Bounding Overwatch</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Bounding overwatch is one of the three movement techniques used in movement formations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soldier">
  <data key="d0">Soldier</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Soldiers carry loads that are critical mission considerations, with recommended weight limits to retain agility and mobility.&lt;SEP&gt;Service members who benefit from IPPS-A efficiency and are equipped with lethal systems like Javelin for operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Soldiers are the primary beneficiaries of Army modernization programs, utilizing systems like HMMWV and weapons.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4a15c71581af907f47533c1630b8096&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503de8a915400d2e62dbb9df698d826a</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\WEAPON_SYSTEMS_HANDBOOK.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778831619</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soldiers' Load">
  <data key="d0">Soldiers' Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The load that Soldiers carry is a planning consideration, recommended to be no more than 30 percent of body weight to maintain effectiveness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assured Mobility">
  <data key="d0">Assured Mobility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Assured mobility is a framework of processes, actions, and capabilities that assures a force's ability to deploy and maneuver to achieve the commander's intent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mobility">
  <data key="d0">Mobility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mobility tasks are combined arms activities that mitigate the effects of obstacles to enable freedom of movement and maneuver.&lt;SEP&gt;A focus area for engineers that is deemphasized during the transition to defensive operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Mobility consists of combined arms activities that mitigate the effects of obstacles to enable freedom of movement and maneuver during a defense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abe3f08fafe8b7389e20d639ffaa8692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-832f2104ac2a57f71576e95a87a29163</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747305</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breaching">
  <data key="d0">Breaching</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Breaching is a primary mobility task conducted to overcome obstacles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clearing">
  <data key="d0">Clearing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Clearing is a primary mobility task involving areas and routes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obstacle">
  <data key="d0">Obstacle</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Obstacles are objects that impede movement, which forces may bypass or breach to maintain momentum.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural or reinforcing feature used to delay, disrupt, or fix enemy forces, often integrated with defensive positions like strong points.&lt;SEP&gt;A barrier used to block, turn, fix, or disrupt enemy movement, which can be natural or reinforcing, and is integrated with fires for effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical barriers emplaced by defending forces to impede avenues of approach and protect critical installations.&lt;SEP&gt;Existing or reinforcing objects, or areas with CBRN contamination, identified during reconnaissance operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d8387d6c63026bf8e7495c788ece1b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069de81fea4e6c37c85bab0a5a971f89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-832f2104ac2a57f71576e95a87a29163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747904</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FM 3-04">
  <data key="d0">FM 3-04</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">FM 3-04 is a field manual providing additional information on the employment of Army aviation in the offense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745992</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FM 3-90-1">
  <data key="d0">FM 3-90-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">FM 3-90-1 is a field manual that describes combat formations and movement techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A field manual referenced for discussions on direct fire control measures and fire support coordination measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16fb9a43ba23b08191569577e00618b7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747294</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indirect Fires">
  <data key="d0">Indirect Fires</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fires used to support close combat and can be supported by unmanned aircraft systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Indirect fires are supported by unmanned aircraft systems to increase the depth and breadth of aviation reconnaissance and maneuver.&lt;SEP&gt;Fires planned and integrated during engagement area development to defeat enemy attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Fire support methods including artillery and mortars used for target marking and illumination.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16fb9a43ba23b08191569577e00618b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a14acea44dac3d3616369b19ec0f2146</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778751447</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reconnaissance Objective">
  <data key="d0">Reconnaissance Objective</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A reconnaissance objective is the target of unmanned systems' loitering activities to gain and maintain enemy contact.&lt;SEP&gt;A terrain feature, geographic area, enemy force, or variable about which a commander wants to obtain additional information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a88d8c8678543b9eb4fa95b4ea87591</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747902</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Staff Planners">
  <data key="d0">Staff Planners</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Staff planners are essential personnel who ensure the effective application and integration of mobility, countermobility, and protection tasks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combat Roads and Trails">
  <data key="d0">Combat Roads and Trails</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Combat roads and trails are infrastructure constructed and maintained as a primary mobility task to enable freedom of movement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forward Airfields">
  <data key="d0">Forward Airfields</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Forward airfields are locations constructed and maintained as a primary mobility task to support aviation operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Landing Zones">
  <data key="d0">Landing Zones</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Landing zones are designated areas constructed and maintained as a primary mobility task for aircraft operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Designated areas where airborne and air assault units deploy, which may present extraction challenges if under direct contact.&lt;SEP&gt;Designated areas established as far forward as possible to meet casualty and evacuation requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Specific areas designated for aircraft to land, often limited in number and requiring reconnaissance in urban terrain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfe3e79800d49e6c15cc7d1d7485cab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb60400466a6008ca4dd586ce73558f</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778751444</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic Management">
  <data key="d0">Traffic Management</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Traffic management is a primary mobility task involving the regulation and enforcement of unit movement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protection">
  <data key="d0">Protection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Protection is a task category integrated within the assured mobility framework to increase the probability of mission accomplishment.&lt;SEP&gt;A function that preserves combat power by synchronizing and integrating protection capabilities and resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The synchronization, integration, and organization of capabilities and resources to protect forces and critical assets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combat Power">
  <data key="d0">Combat Power</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Combat power refers to the effects that are massed in time and space at the decisive point to achieve a purpose.&lt;SEP&gt;Combat power is the total means of destructive and disruptive force that a military unit uses against an enemy, synchronized by commanders.&lt;SEP&gt;Combat power refers to the effects concentrated by commanders at selected locations during an attack.&lt;SEP&gt;The total means of destructive and/or disruptive force a military unit can apply, concentrated through troop movement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e712cfc21b1d5eeb7e94fcc0ac1db7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa0cb96393bbc79e8f283b9593a9806&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Medical Units">
  <data key="d0">Medical Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Units responsible for health service support that must anticipate large numbers of casualties over large geographic areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Units responsible for anticipating and treating casualties over a large geographic area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71131e902d37087dc65113f52eae4eb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weapons Of Mass Destruction">
  <data key="d0">Weapons Of Mass Destruction</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Armaments whose employment greatly increases the number of casualties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mass Casualty Situations">
  <data key="d0">Mass Casualty Situations</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenarios involving casualty numbers that exceed the capabilities of organic and direct support medical assets.&lt;SEP&gt;Events where casualty numbers exceed the capabilities of organic and direct support medical assets.&lt;SEP&gt;Situations for which commanders plan to augment available ambulances and evacuation platforms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71131e902d37087dc65113f52eae4eb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Planners">
  <data key="d0">Planners</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Planners are personnel responsible for a comprehensive array of strategic and operational tasks, primarily focused on urban environments and airspace management. They are tasked with identifying risks and developing agreements necessary for operating within urban airspace. To optimize performance, these individuals must understand aircraft capabilities and consider orbit geometry while integrating escort and escorted forces during operations. Additionally, Planners must consider using all available tools for battle tracking in urban environments to maintain situational awareness.

In terms of civil and environmental considerations, Planners identify boundaries of political units and analyze civil considerations using ASCOPE. They are responsible for considering building characteristics and abiding by rules of engagement during operations. Furthermore, Planners must consider collateral damage reduction strategies and evaluate nonlethal effects, requesting support from appropriate liaison officers when necessary.

Regarding logistical and medical support, Planners anticipate mass casualty possibilities and coordinate with area support medical units. Through these combined efforts involving risk management, civil analysis, and resource coordination, Planners ensure operational effectiveness while adhering to strategic constraints and safety protocols.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8dbf8d99e2395e386c1df16f3ddc745&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c503bd64404512328ab83ccc0ba0997e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32189ba42b4061e42fcfd89b6143133c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39bd2e6c05ba9ec2683ab1b01bd4603d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d765189e92be25ee08b9a34a1f6fb186&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-446c344294ac1157381c690e0f0b5d6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94b0f13f75f0bd64d503a14651482e66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb60400466a6008ca4dd586ce73558f</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778751525</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bases">
  <data key="d0">Bases</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Locations that employ local security measures but may remain vulnerable to bypassed enemy forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Locations used for positioning sustainment units and assets to provide security and support functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soldiers">
  <data key="d0">Soldiers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Personnel who face challenges wearing protective gear and must build confidence in CBRN environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Personnel who must make a psychological adjustment when shifting from advancing to fighting defensively.&lt;SEP&gt;Personnel who are more likely to be rested and capable of prolonged operations if part of uncommitted forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abe3f08fafe8b7389e20d639ffaa8692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a418b78a90bc5acb3f47736189745955</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747301</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CBRN Environments">
  <data key="d0">CBRN Environments</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational settings involving chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear hazards where units must train to perform.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protective Gear">
  <data key="d0">Protective Gear</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Equipment worn by leaders and soldiers that presents challenges in CBRN environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Army Forces">
  <data key="d0">Army Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Army Forces are the military entities that conduct the offense to defeat and destroy enemy forces and gain control of terrain and resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Military organizations that execute combined arms operations for countering weapons of mass destruction.&lt;SEP&gt;Military forces that conduct defensive operations to create conditions favorable for the offense and regain the initiative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa0cb96393bbc79e8f283b9593a9806&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3345760dcbfb2262ee6ddfa43b1677ad</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747304</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Countering Weapons Of Mass Destruction">
  <data key="d0">Countering Weapons Of Mass Destruction</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Operations conducted to curtail the research, development, possession, proliferation, and use of WMDs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integrated Teams">
  <data key="d0">Integrated Teams</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Groups composed of maneuver forces and enablers that conduct tactical countering of weapons of mass destruction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745993</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maneuver Forces">
  <data key="d0">Maneuver Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Elements that combine with enablers to form integrated teams for countering weapons of mass destruction.&lt;SEP&gt;Maneuver Forces are assigned the close area and are supported by Army special operations forces during operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758124</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Detention Facilities">
  <data key="d0">Detention Facilities</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Sites where detainees are collected and interrogation teams are co-located for intelligence gathering.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transition">
  <data key="d0">Transition</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A change in focus from one element of decisive action to another, such as from offensive to defensive operations.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of changing from one type of operation to another, such as from defense to offense or retrograde, requiring planning and agility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Culminating Point">
  <data key="d0">Culminating Point</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A stage in offensive operations where the balance of combat power shifts from the attacking force to the defending force.&lt;SEP&gt;A critical stage in operations where an enemy force is weakened, dispersed, and unprepared for defense.&lt;SEP&gt;The point at which an enemy force can no longer sustain an offensive, often anticipated by commanders to initiate a transition to the offense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a418b78a90bc5acb3f47736189745955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747301</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defensive Operations">
  <data key="d0">Defensive Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Military activities that may be transitioned to when offensive operations reach a culminating point.&lt;SEP&gt;Military operations conducted to consolidate gains, minimize vulnerability, and prepare for future offensive actions.&lt;SEP&gt;Military operations conducted to defeat an enemy attack, gain time, economize forces, and develop conditions favorable for offensive or stability operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Military operations focused on resisting attack, from which a force can transition to offensive or stability operations.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of military operation supported by enabling operations, directed by commanders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abe3f08fafe8b7389e20d639ffaa8692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a418b78a90bc5acb3f47736189745955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3345760dcbfb2262ee6ddfa43b1677ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a88d8c8678543b9eb4fa95b4ea87591</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747903</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Casualty Collection Points">
  <data key="d0">Casualty Collection Points</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Locations identified and designated for the collection of casualties during mass casualty situations.&lt;SEP&gt;Points included in evacuation plans for handling casualties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triage">
  <data key="d0">Triage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A medical process used for the rapid sorting and classification of injured personnel.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Medical Treatment Facilities">
  <data key="d0">Medical Treatment Facilities</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Facilities where injured personnel are evacuated for treatment at the next higher role of care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LOCs">
  <data key="d0">LOCs</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Lines of communication protected by military organizations in an economy-of-force role.&lt;SEP&gt;Lines of Communication for which security may be provided to protect infrastructure.&lt;SEP&gt;LOCs (Lines of Communication) are routes where enemy forces advance and are vulnerable to attack by defending forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Lines of Communication are infrastructure targeted by enemy forces to disrupt the defense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d61f4b5597bdee4add1bccdba2cbd3ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d85f7f19b9f147f37bc741a7c59d700&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747297</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Convoys">
  <data key="d0">Convoys</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Groups of vehicles or personnel transported together, protected by military organizations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radars">
  <data key="d0">Radars</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Critical fixed sites protected by military organizations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Assets">
  <data key="d0">Critical Assets</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Assets protected by commanders through the synchronization of protection capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Assets identified by commanders using decision support tools to prevent or mitigate threats.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operations Security">
  <data key="d0">Operations Security</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A protection task synchronized with other capabilities to protect the force.&lt;SEP&gt;Information-related capabilities synchronized to support the defense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Air And Missile Defense">
  <data key="d0">Air And Missile Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A protection capability essential to protecting the force against aerial threats.&lt;SEP&gt;A modernization priority concerning defense capabilities against aerial and missile threats.&lt;SEP&gt;Air And Missile Defense is a designated modernization priority area for the United States Army.&lt;SEP&gt;Air And Missile Defense is a designated modernization priority of the United States Army.&lt;SEP&gt;A modernization priority focus area for the United States Army to defend against aerial threats.&lt;SEP&gt;A modernization priority area identified for the United States Army.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the six modernization priorities identified by the United States Army for long-range improvement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd09d5c1d391b2d64517a5d72254efc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d02d8715ba139ee7e2b9f2ae7c7d9bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3473302fed80b78e94b810b62c26e3a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15fb7baa23082d86c7d6fc25d3cc0e12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a7ca33dacb79b2456dfa0debe68053e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d121313ed4af44c28824fd63c5a94377</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\WEAPON_SYSTEMS_HANDBOOK.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778831594</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cyberspace And Electronic Warfare Operations">
  <data key="d0">Cyberspace And Electronic Warfare Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Operations integrated with security tasks to protect the force in cyberspace and electromagnetic spectrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Detainees">
  <data key="d0">Detainees</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Individuals captured during offensive operations who are accumulated and held in detention facilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interrogation Teams">
  <data key="d0">Interrogation Teams</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Teams co-located at detention facilities to facilitate the collection of enemy tactical information.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Military Intelligence Personnel">
  <data key="d0">Military Intelligence Personnel</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Personnel responsible for co-locating interrogation teams at detention facilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enablers">
  <data key="d0">Enablers</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Elements combined with maneuver forces to form integrated teams for countering weapons of mass destruction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745994</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CBRN Reconnaissance And Surveillance">
  <data key="d0">CBRN Reconnaissance And Surveillance</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Activities integrated by commanders into overall information collection plans to detect CBRN threats.&lt;SEP&gt;CBRN reconnaissance and surveillance are capabilities integrated into the overall information collection plan by commanders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d85f7f19b9f147f37bc741a7c59d700</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747302</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Information Collection Plans">
  <data key="d0">Information Collection Plans</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Plans into which commanders integrate CBRN reconnaissance and surveillance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LOA">
  <data key="d0">LOA</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Limit of Advance, a line which attacking units reach, potentially halting offensive operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Limit of Advance; a geographical line where a force establishes a defense, potentially leading to a transition to stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a418b78a90bc5acb3f47736189745955</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747301</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operational Pause">
  <data key="d0">Operational Pause</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A planned halt to adjust an operation when objectives cannot be achieved before forces culminate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reserves">
  <data key="d0">Reserves</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Forces available to continue an advance; their absence may cause attacking units to slow or stop.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Casualties">
  <data key="d0">Casualties</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Injured persons anticipated over a large geographic area, particularly in mass casualty situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonstandard Casualty Evacuation Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Nonstandard Casualty Evacuation Procedures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedures that units should plan and rehearse for effective casualty evacuation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Military Organizations">
  <data key="d0">Military Organizations</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Organizations that provide complementary and reinforcing protection capabilities in an economy-of-force role.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3b45e15d5cccdd3d631ceeba2a6d8d0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tempo">
  <data key="d0">Tempo</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The relative speed and rhythm of military operations over time with respect to the enemy, necessary to retain the initiative.&lt;SEP&gt;Tempo is the relative speed and rhythm of military operations over time with respect to the enemy, necessary to retain the initiative.&lt;SEP&gt;The relative speed and rhythm of military operations used to prevent effective enemy counteraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa0cb96393bbc79e8f283b9593a9806</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ADP 3-0">
  <data key="d0">ADP 3-0</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">ADP 3-0 is a reference document cited for the definition of tempo.&lt;SEP&gt;A doctrinal publication referenced in the definition of close combat.&lt;SEP&gt;ADP 3-0 is a doctrinal publication referenced in the sidebar that defines competition.&lt;SEP&gt;ADP 3-0 provides definitions for operational areas such as the close area and deep area.&lt;SEP&gt;Doctrine referenced in the text defining extensive joint combat operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Referenced doctrine regarding stability operations and essential services.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-950f5d02c8283b123e3471016b2f74a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e4dfc214ed288fc6b13f2a08be74357</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758118</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Movement to Contact">
  <data key="d0">Movement to Contact</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Movement to contact is a type of offensive operation designed to develop the situation and establish or regain contact with the enemy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Meeting Engagement">
  <data key="d0">Meeting Engagement</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A meeting engagement is a combat action occurring when an incompletely deployed force engages an enemy at an unexpected time and place.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Search and Attack">
  <data key="d0">Search and Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Search and attack is a subordinate variation of a movement to contact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cordon and Search">
  <data key="d0">Cordon and Search</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cordon and search is a subordinate variation of a movement to contact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attack">
  <data key="d0">Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An attack is a type of offensive operation that destroys or defeats enemy forces, seizes and secures terrain, or both.&lt;SEP&gt;An offensive operation that may be continued by conducting a passage of lines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b036aa74f3adbdabd1d826936e991c47</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ambush">
  <data key="d0">Ambush</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An ambush is a variation of an attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Counterattack">
  <data key="d0">Counterattack</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A Counterattack is defined as a variation of an attack and a specific defensive operation wherein defending forces undertake offensive action against an enemy force. Primarily utilized within area and mobile defenses, this decisive operation aims to destroy enemy forces, restore a position, deny the enemy the ability to exploit success, and regain local superiority. It often functions as a branch or sequel to a broader defensive plan, typically launched before the enemy becomes fully organized.

The execution of a Counterattack is determined by the commander's concept of operations, often involving only a part of the defending force selected for the task. The operation may require a passage of lines to strike the enemy effectively. Successful implementation demands specific mobility support, such as creating breaching lanes or clearing minefields, and requires that sustainment be adequately addressed. Ultimately, it represents a strategic maneuver where defending forces transition to offensive actions to neutralize threats and stabilize the defensive posture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069de81fea4e6c37c85bab0a5a971f89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3345760dcbfb2262ee6ddfa43b1677ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-832f2104ac2a57f71576e95a87a29163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b036aa74f3adbdabd1d826936e991c47</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747968</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Demonstration">
  <data key="d0">Demonstration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A demonstration is a variation of an attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feint">
  <data key="d0">Feint</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A feint is a variation of an attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raid">
  <data key="d0">Raid</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A raid is a variation of an attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745995</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spoiling Attack">
  <data key="d0">Spoiling Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A spoiling attack is a variation of an attack.&lt;SEP&gt;An attack conducted by defending forces to deny the enemy the ability to focus combat power and disrupt preparations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3345760dcbfb2262ee6ddfa43b1677ad</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747304</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exploitation">
  <data key="d0">Exploitation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Exploitation is a type of offensive operation that follows a successful attack to disorganize the enemy in depth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pursuit">
  <data key="d0">Pursuit</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A pursuit is a type of offensive operation designed to catch or cut off a hostile force attempting to escape with the aim of destroying it.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frontal">
  <data key="d0">Frontal</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Frontal is a variation of a pursuit operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combination">
  <data key="d0">Combination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Combination is a variation of a pursuit operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third U.S. Army">
  <data key="d0">Third U.S. Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Third U.S. Army is a military organization whose actions in France during World War II are cited as an example of a pursuit.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="France">
  <data key="d0">France</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">France is the location where the Third U.S. Army conducted pursuit operations between August and September 1944.&lt;SEP&gt;France is the country where the Second Front operations occurred.&lt;SEP&gt;Country where the Second Front was opened and Nazi Germany controlled the West.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="World War II">
  <data key="d0">World War II</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">World War II is the historical event during which the Third U.S. Army conducted pursuit operations in France.&lt;SEP&gt;Global conflict during which the U.S. sought to keep Chinese nationalists fighting Japanese forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bdc5217db0cca10554a11bb5c365968</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758130</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Offensive Control Measures">
  <data key="d0">Common Offensive Control Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Common offensive control measures are used by commanders to synchronize the effects of combat power.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Airspace Coordinating Measures">
  <data key="d0">Airspace Coordinating Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Airspace coordinating measures are control measures used in the offense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745996</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Permissive FSCMs">
  <data key="d0">Permissive FSCMs</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Permissive FSCMs are fire support coordination measures used in the offense.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of fire support coordination measures that includes coordinated fire lines, FSCLs, and free-fire areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16fb9a43ba23b08191569577e00618b7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747294</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Restrictive FSCMs">
  <data key="d0">Restrictive FSCMs</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Restrictive FSCMs are fire support coordination measures used in the offense.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of fire support coordination measures that includes airspace coordination areas, no fire areas, restrictive fire areas, and restrictive fire lines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16fb9a43ba23b08191569577e00618b7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747294</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Initiative">
  <data key="d0">Initiative</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A strategic advantage that commanders seize, retain, and exploit during the conduct of the offense.&lt;SEP&gt;Initiative is the ability to act independently and make decisions, necessary to retain during military operations to maintain tempo.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal advantage of an attacking force that allows commanders to select the time, place, and methods used.&lt;SEP&gt;The operational condition that Army forces seek to regain through the defense to allow for decisive offensive action.&lt;SEP&gt;A strategic advantage or objective that can be regained through operations such as a retrograde.&lt;SEP&gt;The ability to act independently and make decisions; an enemy force possesses this in a defensive scenario, requiring the defending commander to shift efforts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa0cb96393bbc79e8f283b9593a9806&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3345760dcbfb2262ee6ddfa43b1677ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747304</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decisive Operation">
  <data key="d0">Decisive Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An operation that determines the outcome of a major operation, battle, or engagement.&lt;SEP&gt;A decisive operation is an operation that directly accomplishes the mission, for which attacks may be conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;An operation that benefits from the conditions created by the shaping operation.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary operation weighted by air defense coverage to establish a protective corridor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fa0cb96393bbc79e8f283b9593a9806&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71131e902d37087dc65113f52eae4eb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shaping Operation">
  <data key="d0">Shaping Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A shaping operation is an operation that creates conditions for the decisive operation, for which attacks may be conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;An operation that must accomplish its mission to create the conditions for the success of the decisive operation.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of military operation intended to create conditions for a decisive operation, a role often filled by security operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hasty">
  <data key="d0">Hasty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hasty describes a type of operation or attack conducted with minimal planning and preparation time due to immediate circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deliberate">
  <data key="d0">Deliberate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deliberate describes a type of operation or attack conducted with sufficient time for thorough planning and preparation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Command And Control">
  <data key="d0">Command And Control</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Command and control is the exercise of authority and direction by a commander over assigned forces, often targeted by enemy action.&lt;SEP&gt;The process through which commanders, assisted by their staffs, integrate numerous processes and activities to exercise authority and direction.&lt;SEP&gt;The exercise of authority and direction by a properly designated commander over assigned forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adabd6aa6271efdc850feccf7016cdf0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747293</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Division">
  <data key="d0">Division</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Division is a military organizational level, typically involved in planning exploitation operations.&lt;SEP&gt;A command level that uses organic or attached reconnaissance and intelligence elements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a88d8c8678543b9eb4fa95b4ea87591</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747903</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operational Surprise">
  <data key="d0">Operational Surprise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational surprise is a goal achieved during the offense, involving catching the enemy unprepared.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-16d8c89f5caf99f0ea4f94711e809c9b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assault Force">
  <data key="d0">Assault Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A friendly force that assaults an objective, whose freedom of maneuver is increased by the support by fire position.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target">
  <data key="d0">Target</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An area designated and numbered for future firing, or a person, place, or thing against which messages are directed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target Group">
  <data key="d0">Target Group</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A group consisting of two or more targets on which a force may place simultaneous fires.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target Series">
  <data key="d0">Target Series</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A series of targets that are fired in a predetermined time sequence once initiated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Of Attack">
  <data key="d0">Time Of Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The moment the leading elements of the main body cross the line of departure or point of departure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commander">
  <data key="d0">Commander</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Commander is an individual responsible for understanding, visualizing, describing, directing, leading, and assessing operations, and who exercises command and control. This leader is responsible for planning defensive operations, issuing intent, and allocating resources. Additionally, the Commander issues mission orders, defines intent, and ensures synchronization of task-organized elements. As a leader responsible for establishing priorities, designating obstacles, coordinating support, and directing defensive preparations, the Commander also handles requesting support, establishing surveillance, prioritizing risks, and making tactical decisions regarding the defense and counterattacks.

Beyond immediate defensive measures, the Commander is responsible for assessing defense success, planning transitions, and making decisions regarding offensive or retrograde operations. This individual is responsible for assigning missions, conducting operations, and making decisions regarding troop movements and security. Furthermore, the Commander sets the intent, targeting priorities, and may prefer precision-guided munitions to minimize risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adabd6aa6271efdc850feccf7016cdf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069de81fea4e6c37c85bab0a5a971f89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-832f2104ac2a57f71576e95a87a29163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2039fcc410ab417ea95cf892f406c7</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778751548</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commander's Intent">
  <data key="d0">Commander's Intent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A statement that determines the concept of operations and is expanded upon by it.&lt;SEP&gt;A command and control principle that defines the purpose of the operation and the desired end state.&lt;SEP&gt;The commander's intent is a critical element that all elements in the fire support chain must understand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adabd6aa6271efdc850feccf7016cdf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d61f4b5597bdee4add1bccdba2cbd3ff</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747293</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concept Of Operations">
  <data key="d0">Concept Of Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A description of how the commander wants the force to accomplish the mission, expanding on the commander's intent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operations Process">
  <data key="d0">Operations Process</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The process involving the understanding, visualizing, describing, directing, leading, and assessing of tasks by units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brigade Combat Team">
  <data key="d0">Brigade Combat Team</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A subordinate unit that conducts successful combat operations in the close area, supported by higher echelons.&lt;SEP&gt;Modular combined-arms tactical units that receive direct fire support from fixed-wing and rotary aircraft.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cb3d0930de98ddce6ed3ada6a6c40cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\WEAPON_SYSTEMS_HANDBOOK.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778831620</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deep Area">
  <data key="d0">Deep Area</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">An area where corps and divisions conduct operations to create conditions for subordinate units.&lt;SEP&gt;The Deep Area is where operations focus on the enemy's freedom of action and help shape future operations in the close area.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758123</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Fires">
  <data key="d0">Direct Fires</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fires placed on an objective by a unit in a support by fire position to increase the supported force's freedom of maneuver.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target Control Measures">
  <data key="d0">Target Control Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Graphic control measures applied to targets, differing for point, linear, and area targets, which can combine into target series or groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Main Body">
  <data key="d0">Main Body</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The primary force whose leading elements cross the line of departure or point of departure at the time of attack.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal part of a force that moves to advantageous positions while the security force delays the enemy.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary military force that is protected by security operations such as cover, guard, and screen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adabd6aa6271efdc850feccf7016cdf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mission Variables">
  <data key="d0">Mission Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Factors and conditions within a specific area of operations that commanders must understand and address during planning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64c2b09506eccedb4b06b038490a26a5</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Command Post">
  <data key="d0">Command Post</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">A location used by commanders and staffs that can be displaced to terrain protecting from enemy jamming.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Headquarters">
  <data key="d0">Headquarters</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A location from which commanders exercise authority, connected by fiber-optic, telephone cables, and couriers.&lt;SEP&gt;The command element responsible for ordering a linkup and establishing the common operational picture, command relationships, and control measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Headquarters are applicable entities with which Army Special Operations Forces coordinate elements to ensure alignment with corps and division areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758124</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fiber-Optic And Telephone Cables">
  <data key="d0">Fiber-Optic And Telephone Cables</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Physical infrastructure run between headquarters to facilitate communication, though requiring time to establish and displace.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PACE Plan">
  <data key="d0">PACE Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A detailed primary, alternate, contingency, and emergency communication plan directed by commanders to reestablish contact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lower Echelon Units">
  <data key="d0">Lower Echelon Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Units responsible for restoring communications with higher echelons using available resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher Echelon">
  <data key="d0">Higher Echelon</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The superior command entity with which lower echelon units must restore communications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778745998</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enemy Force">
  <data key="d0">Enemy Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Enemy Force is identified as an opposing military entity, organization, or group that commanders seek to disrupt, defeat, or destroy through the application of defensive measures and fires. The primary objective regarding this entity is to degrade its command and control capabilities and prevent the achievement of its mission success. From an operational standpoint, the Enemy Force conducts attacks and reconnaissance against defending forces, attempting to break through the Main Battle Area (MBA), attack along boundaries, infiltrate lines, or execute airborne and air assault operations. It is also capable of exploiting limited visibility to land or conduct attacks and possesses advanced capabilities, including air, satellite, and unmanned aircraft systems that pose a threat to logistic operations.

The presence of an Enemy Force necessitates that defending units undertake survivability operations and specific defensive preparations to counter its aggression. While it poses a significant threat, this opposing force is also subject to tactical vulnerabilities. As it maneuvers, the Enemy Force may become dispersed, extended in depth, or overextended, particularly as it approaches a culminating point where it is weakened. These conditions provide opportunities for friendly forces to engage the enemy units effectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adabd6aa6271efdc850feccf7016cdf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16fb9a43ba23b08191569577e00618b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069de81fea4e6c37c85bab0a5a971f89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a418b78a90bc5acb3f47736189745955&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-832f2104ac2a57f71576e95a87a29163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4e12f4d30fc2f3059510d3457b00e7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747336</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Close Combat">
  <data key="d0">Close Combat</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Warfare carried out on land in a direct-fire fight to defeat or destroy enemy forces, often through shock effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6bf6abab46637a933d4adcc9ec578f4</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
