  <data key="d0">Survivability Positions</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Survivability positions are constructed locations such as trenches, command post shelters, and fighting positions designed to protect personnel and equipment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Engineer Units">
  <data key="d0">Engineer Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Engineer units assist maneuver and supporting units in preparing survivability positions and field fortifications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BCT Engineer Battalions">
  <data key="d0">BCT Engineer Battalions</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">BCT engineer battalions possess specific capabilities for engineer support, though requirements beyond their capabilities are passed to higher headquarters.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Army Maneuver Enhancement Brigade">
  <data key="d0">Army Maneuver Enhancement Brigade</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">An Army maneuver enhancement brigade is a higher headquarters unit that supports divisions or corps when BCT engineer capabilities are exceeded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dispersion">
  <data key="d0">Dispersion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dispersion is a defensive technique used to limit damage from enemy attacks by spreading out unit personnel and equipment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cover">
  <data key="d0">Cover</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cover is a method of protection utilizing natural terrain features like folds in the earth and trees, or constructed fortifications like digging in.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of security operation conducted independently to protect the main body by fighting to gain time and preventing enemy observation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747904</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CBRN Defense">
  <data key="d0">CBRN Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CBRN defense involves plans and measures established to protect personnel and equipment from chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear hazards.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transportation Units">
  <data key="d0">Transportation Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Transportation units are military organizations that can, in some circumstances, provide their own security for movements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concealment">
  <data key="d0">Concealment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Concealment is a method used by units to enhance survivability by avoiding detection and destruction by enemy forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea48c62cf850e8a658d6027f583efe1</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747310</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Commanders Intent">
  <data key="d0">Commanders Intent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A command and control principle that defines the limits within which subordinates may exercise initiative and provides confidence in ambiguous situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disciplined Initiative">
  <data key="d0">Disciplined Initiative</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Action taken by subordinates in the absence of orders to react to unforeseen enemy actions or opportunities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subordinates">
  <data key="d0">Subordinates</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Personnel who exercise disciplined initiative and adjust to new situations to achieve the commander's intent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defensive Mission">
  <data key="d0">Defensive Mission</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A military assignment that generally imposes few restrictions on a defending commander while requiring prevention of enemy penetration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defending Commander">
  <data key="d0">Defending Commander</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A commander conducting a defensive mission who allocates resources, shifts efforts, and manages reserves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Control Measures">
  <data key="d0">Control Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Measures such as contact points and phase lines used to coordinate flank and rear activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shaping Operations">
  <data key="d0">Shaping Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Operations conducted to contain enemy attacks until the defending force can seize the initiative.&lt;SEP&gt;Operations conducted to attrit, disrupt, and delay the enemy to establish conditions for decisive operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Operations consisting of enabling operations conducted by commanders to create conditions for decisive operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a88d8c8678543b9eb4fa95b4ea87591</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747903</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Task Organization">
  <data key="d0">Task Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The arrangement of forces and resources to respond to existing or projected situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Operating Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Standard Operating Procedures</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Established procedures employed by elements of a task-organized unit to ensure understanding and synchronization.&lt;SEP&gt;Established procedures required to obtain proficiency in the conduct of security operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03ce33eb77548e57e227d14350711715</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Command Posts">
  <data key="d0">Command Posts</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Static facilities placed in hardened terrain to reduce electronic and visual signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defensive Positions">
  <data key="d0">Defensive Positions</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Locations occupied by defending forces to engage attackers, often reinforced by obstacles.&lt;SEP&gt;Locations occupied by units that achieve mutual support through observation, fires, and patrol activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747311</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Counterattacks">
  <data key="d0">Counterattacks</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Offensive actions taken by a defending unit to restore defensive integrity if a position is overrun.&lt;SEP&gt;Offensive actions conducted to regain the initiative or destroy enemy forces after an enemy success.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="COA">
  <data key="d0">COA</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Course of Action; disciplined initiative may offer a greater chance of success than the original COA.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communications Nodes">
  <data key="d0">Communications Nodes</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Facilities that tend to be relatively static in the defense and are placed in hardened areas to reduce signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Battlefield">
  <data key="d0">Battlefield</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The area of conflict which commanders may shape by defending in one area and delaying in another.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12d54164de431a3e56787d0f04dea3cf</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Operational Picture">
  <data key="d0">Common Operational Picture</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A shared display of relevant information established by headquarters to coordinate operations and situational awareness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retrograde Operations">
  <data key="d0">Retrograde Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Operations conducted when a defense is unsuccessful, allowing a force to preserve combat capability and establish conditions for future success.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mobile Defense">
  <data key="d0">Mobile Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mobile Defense is a type of defensive operation that concentrates on the destruction or defeat of the enemy through a decisive attack by a striking force.&lt;SEP&gt;A defensive method relying on a striking force to generate overwhelming combat power at a decisive point.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of defense where transitioning to the offense generally follows a striking force's attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747312</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Striking Force">
  <data key="d0">Striking Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Striking Force is a dedicated counterattack force in a mobile defense constituted with the bulk of available combat power.&lt;SEP&gt;A decisive force used in mobile defense to generate overwhelming combat power against the enemy.&lt;SEP&gt;A designated unit used in a mobile defense whose attack typically precedes the transition to offensive operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Window Of Opportunity">
  <data key="d0">Window Of Opportunity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A timeframe identified by a defending commander to transition to the offense when the enemy is overextended.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recognition Signals">
  <data key="d0">Recognition Signals</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Signals established by headquarters to ensure positive identification between converging forces during a linkup.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communication Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Communication Procedures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedures established by headquarters to coordinate radio traffic and information exchange during linkup operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ac9465c9d545f87eb3a80e932a498f3</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Battle of Kursk">
  <data key="d0">Battle of Kursk</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Battle of Kursk is a historical event in July 1943 that serves as an example of an area defense conducted by the Soviets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Soviets">
  <data key="d0">Soviets</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Soviets are a military force that conducted an area defense during the Battle of Kursk in July 1943.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fixing Force">
  <data key="d0">Fixing Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A Fixing Force is a designated unit that supplements the striking force by preventing the enemy from moving from a specific area and canalizing them into ambush areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="German General Manstein">
  <data key="d0">German General Manstein</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">German General Manstein is a historical military leader who conducted a mobile defense during the Donbas Operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Donbas Operation">
  <data key="d0">Donbas Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Donbas Operation is a historical military operation in Ukraine in February 1943 that serves as an example of a mobile defense.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ukraine">
  <data key="d0">Ukraine</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Ukraine is the geographical location where the Donbas Operation took place in February 1943.&lt;SEP&gt;Country that received military assistance and transformed its special operations capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6e82af28ec6fa5c74054f474ea4690</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758120</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Withdrawal">
  <data key="d0">Withdrawal</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Withdrawal is a variation of retrograde where a force disengages from an enemy force and moves in a direction away from the enemy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ef422907967635431abf4581ffee5bb8</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Survivability Move">
  <data key="d0">Survivability Move</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A tactical method involving the rapid displacement of a unit, command post, or facility in response to direct and indirect fires, threats, or intelligence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747314</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spoiling Attacks">
  <data key="d0">Spoiling Attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A preemptive offensive operation conducted to disrupt enemy troop concentrations and attack preparations before an offensive can be launched.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747314</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Armored and Stryker Forces">
  <data key="d0">Armored and Stryker Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Military units equipped with armored combat vehicles that utilize tactical mobility and protection for defense and security operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747314</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Armored Combat Vehicles">
  <data key="d0">Armored Combat Vehicles</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Vehicles that provide tactical mobility, protection, and the capability to maneuver in dynamic and static defenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-43a1cd0ab6021273635c13e3b7af3476</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34828-ADP_3-90-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778747314</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Airborne and Air Assault Units">
