</node>
<node id="Conventional Forces">
  <data key="d0">Conventional Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Military forces distinct from special operations forces, for whom Army special operations forces may set conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Army special operations forces support conventional forcible entry operations and conventional warfare.&lt;SEP&gt;Conventional Forces are supported by Army Special Operations Forces through enhanced C5ISRT capabilities to maneuver against enemy forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Conventional forces transition to legitimate government authorities and retrograde personnel and equipment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ada7ac41ab629e1f7809edf7d62800b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a0c67aeaade5d9ae17a53e58e80e73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660d19754e7a8b4d28766ae683085446</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S.-led Coalition">
  <data key="d0">U.S.-led Coalition</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The broader coalition force during Operation Iraqi Freedom that included Task Force Viking.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ada7ac41ab629e1f7809edf7d62800b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oil Production Facilities">
  <data key="d0">Oil Production Facilities</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategic facilities in northern Iraq that Task Force Viking aimed to secure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ada7ac41ab629e1f7809edf7d62800b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Operations Task Force">
  <data key="d0">Special Operations Task Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A command structure (O-5) that Army special operations forces may establish during crisis response.&lt;SEP&gt;A Special Operations Task Force is a command structure established by Army Special Operations Forces as a lead element of a crisis response force.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ada7ac41ab629e1f7809edf7d62800b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660d19754e7a8b4d28766ae683085446</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Front">
  <data key="d0">Second Front</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Second Front refers to the Allied invasion launched on 6 June 1944 in Normandy, France.&lt;SEP&gt;Opened by the Allies on 6 June 1944 in Normandy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normandy">
  <data key="d0">Normandy</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Normandy is the region in France where the Second Front landing operations took place.&lt;SEP&gt;Location in France where the Allies opened the Second Front.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S. Army">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. Army had the task of seizing and holding beaches during the Second Front.&lt;SEP&gt;Had the task of seizing and holding the beaches code-named UTAH and OMAHA.&lt;SEP&gt;Formed an all-volunteer provisional force designated the 5307th Composite Unit (Provisional) during World War II.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Army utilizes systems like IBCS and manages depots for chemical weapon destruction programs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bdc5217db0cca10554a11bb5c365968&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adaee111f19e7fd13f315f0ca1141b82</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\WEAPON_SYSTEMS_HANDBOOK.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778831639</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Utah Beach">
  <data key="d0">Utah Beach</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Utah Beach was one of the beached code-named UTAH targeted for seizure by the U.S. Army.&lt;SEP&gt;Code-named beach the U.S. Army had the task of seizing and holding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Omaha Beach">
  <data key="d0">Omaha Beach</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Omaha Beach was one of the beached code-named OMAHA targeted for seizure by the U.S. Army.&lt;SEP&gt;Code-named beach the U.S. Army had the task of seizing and holding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758126</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pointe du Hoc">
  <data key="d0">Pointe du Hoc</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Pointe du Hoc is a promontory between Utah and Omaha beaches where German guns were believed to be emplaced.&lt;SEP&gt;Promontory between Utah and Omaha beaches where Germans were believed to have emplaced large guns.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="German Forces">
  <data key="d0">German Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">German Forces were believed to have emplaced large guns at Pointe du Hoc capable of firing on landing zones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Large Guns">
  <data key="d0">Large Guns</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Large guns were believed to be emplaced by German Forces at Pointe du Hoc to interdict beach landings.&lt;SEP&gt;Capable of firing on the approaches and landing zones of both beaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S. Army Rangers">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Army Rangers</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">U.S. Army Rangers received the mission of seizing and holding Pointe du Hoc due to their elite infantry reputation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Provisional Ranger Force">
  <data key="d0">Provisional Ranger Force</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Provisional Ranger Force was organized to take Pointe du Hoc and was commanded by Lieutenant Colonel James E. Rudder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="James E. Rudder">
  <data key="d0">James E. Rudder</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lieutenant Colonel James E. Rudder commanded the Provisional Ranger Force and led Force A.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2nd Ranger Battalion">
  <data key="d0">2nd Ranger Battalion</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The 2nd Ranger Battalion was part of the Provisional Ranger Force split into three groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="5th Ranger Battalion">
  <data key="d0">5th Ranger Battalion</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The 5th Ranger Battalion was part of the Provisional Ranger Force and operated under LTC Max F. Schneider in Force C.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force A">
  <data key="d0">Force A</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Force A was composed of Companies D, E, and F of the 2nd Rangers and scaled the cliffs at Pointe du Hoc.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force B">
  <data key="d0">Force B</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Force B consisted of Company C, 2nd Battalion, and landed at Charlie Sector on Omaha Beach.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758127</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Force C">
  <data key="d0">Force C</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Force C was composed of Companies A and B, 2nd Rangers, and the 5th Rangers, landing at Dog White Sector.</data>
