<node id="FM 3-07">
  <data key="d0">FM 3-07</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">FM 3-07 is a doctrinal reference addressing mission sets involving stability operations and crisis response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-660d19754e7a8b4d28766ae683085446</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778758138</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Army Special Operations Forces">
  <data key="d0">Army Special Operations Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Army Special Operations Forces are primary U.S. Army military units discussed in FM 3-05 responsible for conducting special operations activities across a spectrum of strategic contexts, including competition, crisis, and armed conflict. These forces transition between these contexts to achieve policy goals, maintain stability, and deter conflict through flexible response or deterrence operations. During crises, they act to counter provocation, deter escalation, and resolve threats, while also participating in stability operations and transitions back to competition. Their role encompasses conducting operations throughout the operational framework in armed conflict to support broader national objectives.

In terms of operational execution, Army Special Operations Forces operate in rear, close, and deep areas to support conventional forces and contribute to large-scale combat operations. They serve as a primary military organization conducting operations in deep areas to support joint force objectives and disrupt enemy capabilities. By working with partners to add operational depth, they support conventional operations while maintaining the ability to disrupt enemy cohesion. This is achieved by targeting systems that enable enemy situational awareness and coordinated action, thereby enhancing the effectiveness of the joint force during conflict.

Furthermore, Army Special Operations Forces engage deeply with local partners and understand partner nation culture to facilitate cooperation. They provide stay behind forces when necessary and work collaboratively to ensure operational success. Their activities are designed to add operational depth and support the broader joint force objectives during armed conflict and other strategic phases. Through these diverse activities, Army Special Operations Forces ensure flexible response capabilities that align with policy goals across all phases of competition and conflict.&lt;SEP&gt;Army Special Operations Forces operate two Gray Eagle Extended Range systems and receive multimission UAS capabilities from the MQ-1C.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-950f5d02c8283b123e3471016b2f74a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d264de22c88d1296cd175906c25ab76b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6e82af28ec6fa5c74054f474ea4690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ada7ac41ab629e1f7809edf7d62800b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bdc5217db0cca10554a11bb5c365968&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a0c67aeaade5d9ae17a53e58e80e73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e4dfc214ed288fc6b13f2a08be74357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a75fd9bf2d8ffd7deb7994794314ba94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660d19754e7a8b4d28766ae683085446&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6faa94aab2e13591315f79ebbd49ab0</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;D:\Academic resources\WEAPON_SYSTEMS_HANDBOOK.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778831624</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FM 3-05">
  <data key="d0">FM 3-05</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">FM 3-05 is an Army Field Manual published on 26 June 2025 that serves as the doctrinal reference for Army Special Operations Forces. This military manual, formally titled "Army Special Operations Forces During Competition, Crisis, and Armed Conflict," outlines the doctrines and activities applicable to these forces. The document details Army Special Operations Forces capabilities and operational conduct during competition, crisis, and armed conflict. As a publication dated 26 June 2025, it provides comprehensive text on the doctrines governing special operations.

The manual contains specific doctrine regarding security assistance and stability tasks, documenting the full range of Army Special Operations Forces responsibilities. While one description characterizes FM 3-05 as a Field Manual chapter discussing Special Operations During Armed Conflict, the majority identify it as the standalone manual detailing these forces during competition, crisis, and armed conflict. The document integrates guidance on activities during competition and conflict, ensuring clarity on Army Special Operations Forces doctrine. FM 3-05 remains the key Army manual documenting special operations forces doctrine and stability tasks for the specified operational periods.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-950f5d02c8283b123e3471016b2f74a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d264de22c88d1296cd175906c25ab76b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6e82af28ec6fa5c74054f474ea4690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d90513532ac94b30a700a8204e509e17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ada7ac41ab629e1f7809edf7d62800b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c69d05b2f34d86a1867e7e7939634802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bdc5217db0cca10554a11bb5c365968&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3a0c67aeaade5d9ae17a53e58e80e73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa8d93750f6d13a9825215c8b00f7149&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e4dfc214ed288fc6b13f2a08be74357&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a75fd9bf2d8ffd7deb7994794314ba94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-660d19754e7a8b4d28766ae683085446</data>
